 own lists of people  that they won't have on board anymore  delta said it had put nearly 1900 people  on its list for refusing to comply with  masking requirements and submitted more  than 900 ban names to the tsa to pursue  civil penalties  the concern is though that they don't  and really can't share that information  so somebody has caused a problem on a  united flight  and they can no longer fly united  because of the airlines policies you  know in theory they can book their next  flight on delta where they may end up  being disrupted again delta said that  while such incidents of bad behavior  represent just a small fraction of the  overall flights on delta the rate of  incidence on the airline has increased  nearly 100 percent since 2019. flight  attendants are absolutely not willing to  accept this as the new normal so what  they're saying is make it stop you also  see this in the fact that across the  board their flight attendants are just  not willing to pick up the voluntary  overtime hours that they were before the  pandemic and so the public saw that in  many flight cancellations in the fall  and even over the holidays in 2021 the  faa said it initiated nearly 1100  investigations into early passengers  which is more than the previous seven  years combined flight attendants will  say a lot of these incidents don't  necessarily have anything to do with  masks you know some of them involve  alcohol and some of them just seem to  defy all explanation  the binding administration has voiced  its support for the idea anything  besides zero is an unacceptable rate  but not everyone is in favor of a  national no-fly list  recently a group of republican senators  wrote a letter to attorney general mayor  garland suggesting that such a law could  infringe on civil rights the republican  senators who oppose the letter you know  basically they argue that it would give  the department of justice too much  authority to create a list and there  wouldn't necessarily be